            <div xml:id="d882">

                <pb facs="1024" n="906" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg906"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.11173 Barley/17: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Poole</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-10-05T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 5,
                                1928—noon</hi>.</date><lb/>[Received October 5—10
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>202. My 197, September 28, 1 p.m. Foreign Office states barley situation growing
                    worse and absolute embargo may be laid down. German importers have decided not
                    to take up documents on arriving shipments which seems a clear breach of
                    contract. American exporters should at once consider feasibility of sending a
                    competent representative here, possibly from London, with whom Embassy can
                    cooperate in looking after their legal and other interests. If this is
                    communicated to the Department of Agriculture please say Steere<anchor n="50"
                        xml:id="d882-f1"/> joins in recommendation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Poole</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d882-f1" n="50" xml:id="d882-f1-content">
                <p>Representative in Germany of the Department of Agriculture.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
